          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Sinusoidal signals and phase",
          "content": "# Unit 1, Lesson 1: Sinusoidal signals and phase\n\n**Primary relation:** `v(t)=Vm cos(2\u03c0ft+\u03c6)`  \n**Unit outcome:** Represent a time-varying steady-state circuit with complex algebra without confusing phasors with physical time signals.\n\n## What the relation means\n\nAmplitude, phase, frequency, and time shift determine the complete sinusoid. A phase lead is a shift toward earlier maxima when waveforms share the same frequency. The expression `v(t)=Vm cos(2\u03c0ft+\u03c6)` is meaningful only when the input, output, reference arrows, and component idealizations are stated. Draw one simple circuit or signal diagram, label every referenced terminal, and identify which terms can be measured and which are inferred from the model. Distinguish an algebraic sign from a physical polarity; a sign reversal after changing an arrow is usually a coordinate choice, not a violation of energy conservation.\n\n## Detailed engineering reasoning\n\nThe first bridge from Circuit Analysis I is a change of representation: from a changing voltage trace to a complex constant at one selected frequency. Students should draw at least one real time-axis waveform beside each phasor computation and identify the convention. A phase angle does not give a physical time until frequency is specified. When two different tones are present, analyze each independently and superpose in the time domain.\n\nFor **sinusoidal signals and phase**, begin with a simplest-case prediction before substituting values. If an excitation vanishes, a passive zero-initial-state model must not invent an output. If one parameter becomes very large or small, interpret the corresponding limit with the relevant source and load held fixed. For an AC circuit, a magnitude-only answer is incomplete whenever phase matters, and for a transient circuit the initial state changes the waveform even if the final DC steady state is the same.\n\n### Worked example and interpretation\n\nA 6 V peak waveform at 50 Hz peaks at t=0; a 90\u00b0-lagging copy peaks 5 ms later. Start from the stated equation, convert any milli-, micro- or kilo- prefixes to SI units, and substitute only quantities that share a consistent peak/RMS convention. Report the numerical quantity with physical units. Then explain what the sign or phase of the result tells an observer. Use another representation\u2014for example rectangular versus polar complex notation or time versus frequency response\u2014to check the result without simply repeating the first arithmetic.\n\nThe model predicts a *conditional* answer. It does not predict tolerance drift, insulation integrity, wiring parasitics, thermal runaway, bandwidth of an instrument, or safe operation unless those effects have been explicitly included. A nominal value should therefore be reported alongside its assumed operating conditions.\n\n### Failure mode to detect\n\nTwo sinusoids of different frequencies cannot be added by adding fixed phasors. To diagnose this error, ask which assumption was silently changed. Trace the computation back to the definition of the source, the selected terminals, and the units of the constitutive law. A useful check is to repeat the calculation with a parameter changed by a factor of two and decide whether the direction of the output change is consistent with physical intuition. Where the network has several branches, apply KCL, KVL, and a power balance rather than trusting a single number.\n\n### Design exercise: a three-stage method\n\n1. **Prediction.** Before any calculator is used, describe the expected response for low, nominal, and high excitation. Write down the reference direction and the output to measure.\n2. **Calculation.** Derive the governing expression from the ideal element relations. Recalculate a second operating point and show intermediate values, SI conversions, and the choice of complex argument branch if needed.\n3. **Assessment.** Identify a parasitic or nonideality absent from the calculation. Explain how an engineer would test it with a safe low-voltage setup, without assuming that software itself validates a built device.\n\n### Problems\n\n- **Conceptual:** Explain in your own words why `v(t)=Vm cos(2\u03c0ft+\u03c6)` is appropriate for the stated system and list two situations where the result would not apply without modification.\n- **Quantitative:** Rework the numerical example after increasing one *independent* component value by 20%; explicitly state which variables remain fixed, calculate the altered result, and explain whether a first-order sensitivity estimate would work.\n- **Verification:** Construct an independent check based on dimensions, a limiting condition, a KCL/KVL residual, or a conservation law. If it disagrees with the direct calculation, identify the first step where the models diverge.\n- **Extension:** Propose a simulation with three parameter values and a graph that could reveal a modeling assumption. Mark the horizontal/vertical units and explain the limits of the conclusion.\n\n### Connection to the wider book\n\nThis topic is a building block for analyzing networks in which one part shapes phase, another changes loading, and a measurement stage perturbs the circuit. The central habit is to distinguish a device's constitutive relation from the system-level network constraints. In later units the same pattern appears in three-phase neutral currents, coupled coils, transfer functions, and power-quality measurement. Keeping a consistent reference convention across these representations prevents apparently contradictory results.\n"
